    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm working through a new assemblage from a Late Helladic IIIC stratum. I'm having some trouble distinguishing between certain slip applications on these ceramic shards. Some look like they were intentionally burnished, but others just seem to have a weird, uneven texture that's hard to categorize. Can you help me figure out how to differentiate between intentional slip/burnishing and variations in the firing process that might cause similar surface effects?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To differentiate, examine the slip's uniformity and sheen; intentionally burnished slips will typically have a smooth, lustrous finish with consistent pressure marks. Conversely, uneven textures caused by firing variations often appear more erratic, perhaps showing areas of blistering or mineral deposits from inconsistent kiln atmosphere. Look closely for micro-abrasions or distinct directional sheen, which strongly suggests careful hand-finishing over random thermal effects.",
      "source": "target",
