    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I've been looking at the blistering you mentioned, but some of these fragments are so small and scattered throughout the site that it's hard to tell if it's a systematic firing issue or just local soil chemistry affecting the surface. When you're dealing with such highly fragmented material, are there any specific taphonomic indicators or residues that can help confirm if the surface degradation happened post-deposition or during the original firing?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Taphonomic analysis requires looking for physical evidence of post-depositional damage, such as rounding, lichen growth, or abrasive patination from burial chemistry. Residues revealing the original temper or firing atmosphere, like embedded organic matter or specific mineral leaching patterns, can help distinguish in-situ versus post-depositional alteration. If the degradation is purely chemical in the soil, it often involves dissolution or staining that is not related to the ceramic body's structure.",
      "source": "target",
